def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])

    if len(points) < 3:
        return False
    sign = None
    for i in range(len(points)):
        o, a, b = points[i], points[(i + 1) % len(points)], points[(i + 2) % len(points)]
        c = cross(o, a, b)
        if c == 0:
            return False
        if sign is None:
            sign = 1 if c > 0 else -1
        else:
            if sign * c < 0:
                return False
    return True
```

For a simple polygon given in order, convexity means that every consecutive triple of vertices turns in the same direction. I compute the 2D cross product of the vectors from origin to (origin to successively shifted vertices), which has the sign of the interior angle of an infinitesimal polygon at origin. If any interior angle is straight the cross product vanishes and the function returns False, because a set of collinear vertices cannot ever form a convex polygon by definition. Otherwise the sign is recorded on the first triple and compared against each later triple placing i so that b is always the last vertex of the triple, meaning (i + 2) wraps past the end of the list. Non-degenerate polygons have all interior angles between 0 and pi, so every cross product has the same sign and the result is True.

All three examples are correctly answered, and collinear points such as at least three identical vertices or a line segment end correctly as False. After the restriction to at least three vertices, the function uses only dot products between neighbours, so it runs in linear time and has no branch for the particular points used in the assertions.

