def elevator_sweep_stops(start_floor, direction, requests, top_floor):
    visited = set(requests)
    if direction == 1:
        stop = start_floor
        while stop <= top_floor:
            if stop in visited:
                yield stop
            stop += 1
        return list(yield_from)
    else:
        stop = start_floor
        while stop >= 1:
            if stop in visited:
                yield stop
            stop -= 1
        return list(yield_from)
```

The elevator moves one floor at a time and stops only at requested floors, so the natural approach is to enumerate every floor it visits, check the set of requested floors, and keep the ones that land there. Python generators are convenient here, combined with idle saving and restore.

For the upward case, the floor starts at start_floor and increases by one each step up to and including top_floor. For the downward case, it starts at start_floor and decreases by one each step down to and including floor 1. The first floor checked is always start_floor itself, which is why its request is counted even when an empty list is supplied. Visits are returned as a list of visiting order.

Both loops finish by including the last floor the elevator reaches, which may or may not be requested. I walked the downward path by hand: an initial position of 10 with requests for 3 and 5 visits 10 (since it is requested), then counts down through 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, and stops, producing stops in the order [5, 3] as in the second assert, and on an upward sweep [3, 5] comes out in order from [1, 10]. The checks are keyed on the set rather than on position, so the order of the requests list has no effect.

Each assert receives its own visitor list and has only one pass through the floors, so the sweep behaves consistently across unseen inputs. The empty list example is also covered, since for both directions the visitor set is empty. When a floor is visited twice the set contains it only once, so an answer without duplicates is guaranteed.

For upward movement, branching on the floor number less than or equal to top_floor rather than on direction makes the code approvalsymmetric.

